We recognize iris texture using Fisher linear discriminant analysis (LDA) and
nearest-center classification. An image passes through:
Grayscale image -> iris boundaries -> rectangular strip -> enhanced texture
-> 1,536 features -> Fisher projection -> comparison with known eyes.

Libraries: OpenCV (opencv-python, cv2) processes images; NumPy (numpy, np)
handles arrays/math; scikit-learn (sklearn) supplies LDA and ROC calculations;

Step 1: Read an image and keep training separate from testing

IrisRecognition.py defines:
    load_dataset(data_dir=DATA_DIR) -> (train, test)
    extract_features(samples, angles) -> (features, labels)
    run_experiment(data_dir=DATA_DIR) -> results dictionary
    main() -> None

load_dataset() returns (path, eye ID) pairs: session 1 contains 324 training
images, session 2 contains 432 test images, across 108 eyes. Separate sessions
test recognition on later captures. main() starts run_experiment(), which calls
extract_features() and then evaluates recognition.

    cv2.imread(path, cv2.IMREAD_GRAYSCALE) -> 280x320 brightness array
For each image, extract_features() runs steps 2-5. Training uses seven starting
angles (-9,-6,-3,0,3,6,9 degrees) to accommodate eye rotation; testing uses zero.
Features reach matching as training (324,7,1536) and testing (432,1536) arrays.

Step 2: Locate the iris so surrounding regions do not dominate recognition

    iris_localization(image, window_size=120) -> (pupil, iris)
Each circle is (center_x, center_y, radius). In IrisLocalization.py, the darkest
row/column estimates the pupil center. Two refinement passes select the largest
dark component in a 120x120 window; its centroid and sqrt(area/pi) define the pupil.

    cv2.GaussianBlur(image, (5,5), 0) -> smoothed image: suppresses noise.
    cv2.threshold(window, 0, 255, type) -> (threshold, mask): Otsu plus
        inverted-binary flags automatically separate dark foreground pixels.
    cv2.connectedComponentsWithStats(mask) -> (count, labels, stats, centroids):
        groups connected pixels and measures areas/centers.
    cv2.HoughCircles(image, method, dp, minDist, ...) -> circles or None:
        finds circular boundaries using HOUGH_GRADIENT here.
Choose the outer circle nearest the pupil within radii 80-130 pixels; fall back
to the pupil center and radius 105 if none is detected.

Step 3: Unwrap the ring to give different images comparable coordinates

    iris_normalization(image, pupil, iris, rows=64, cols=512, angle_deg=0)
        -> float32 array (64,512)
IrisNormalization.py samples angles along columns and interpolates from pupil
to outer boundary along rows. angle_deg changes the starting rotation. NumPy
arange(), sin(), cos(), and deg2rad() construct the coordinate maps.

    cv2.remap(image, map_x, map_y, interpolation, borderMode=...) -> sampled image
Bilinear interpolation fills the strip; BORDER_REPLICATE handles out-of-image
coordinates. Pupil and iris circles may have different centers.

Step 4: Correct lighting to make texture differences easier to compare

    image_enhancement(normalized, background_block=16, histogram_block=32)
        -> uint8 array (64,512)
ImageEnhancement.py estimates illumination using 16x16 block means, enlarges
and subtracts it, then improves contrast independently in each 32x32 block.

    array.mean(axis) -> averages over the selected axis.
    cv2.resize(background, (width,height), interpolation=cv2.INTER_CUBIC)
        -> full-sized illumination estimate.
    cv2.normalize(corrected, None, 0, 255, cv2.NORM_MINMAX, dtype=cv2.CV_8U)
        -> values rescaled to the uint8 range.
    cv2.equalizeHist(block) -> histogram-equalized block with improved contrast.

Step 5: Describe the texture with numbers

    feature_extraction(enhanced, roi_rows=48, block_size=8,
                       sigmas=((3.0,1.5),(4.5,1.5)), frequencies=None)
        -> float32 vector (1536,)
FeatureExtraction.py keeps the 48 rows nearest the pupil to reduce outer-region
occlusions. Two Gaussian/radial-cosine filters capture texture at different scales.
frequencies=None selects the chosen frequencies 1/8 and 1/16 cycles/pixel.

    np.meshgrid(x, y) -> coordinate grids for constructing the kernels.
    cv2.filter2D(roi, cv2.CV_32F, kernel, borderType=cv2.BORDER_REFLECT_101)
        -> texture responses, using reflected image edges.
Each 8x8 response block contributes mean magnitude and mean absolute deviation:
these measure texture strength and variation. Two filters x 384 blocks x two
statistics = 1,536 features. The deviation is not standard deviation.

Step 6: Learn what distinguishes eyes, then match a test image

    iris_matching(train_features, train_labels, test_features, n_components=107)
        -> (classes, distances)
IrisMatching.py learns a smaller feature space that separates different eyes
relative to variation within an eye. With 108 classes, Fisher permits at most
107 discriminant directions.

    sklearn.discriminant_analysis.LinearDiscriminantAnalysis(
        solver="svd", n_components=k) -> LDA estimator
    lda.fit(X, y) -> fitted estimator; lda.scalings_ stores projection directions.
reshape() combines training images/rotations into 2,268 rows; np.repeat() repeats
the labels accordingly. Only training data fits LDA. Both datasets are projected
using the selected directions and matrix multiplication.

Average the three training images per eye separately at each rotation, obtaining
seven reference centers. Compare a test vector f against every center c:
    L1 = sum(abs(f-c))
    Squared L2 = sum((f-c)**2)
    Cosine distance = 1 - dot(f,c)/(norm(f)*norm(c))
np.linalg.norm() computes vector lengths; np.clip() bounds similarity to [-1,1].
Keep the minimum over rotations, then predict the closest eye. Each distance
matrix is (432,108). n_components=None compares original features without LDA.

Step 7: Evaluate identification and verification
    performance_evaluation(train_features, train_labels, test_features,
                           test_labels, dimensions=(20,40,60,80,100,107))
        -> dictionary of CRRs, thresholds, ROC data, and figures
PerformanceEvaluation.py uses argmin(axis=1) to predict the nearest eye and
100*mean(predicted == test_labels) to obtain CRR. It compares original and
107-dimensional features for all three distances and plots cosine CRR across k.



Limitations and improvements
The method may struggle with blurry images, uneven lighting, or eyes partly
covered by eyelids or eyelashes. The current localization code assumes that the
largest dark area in the search window is the pupil and chooses the outer iris
circle mainly by how close its center is to the pupil center. Shadows or eyelashes
can confuse these choices, while the fixed size limits and default radius used
when detection fails may not fit every image. Incorrect boundaries can cause
later steps to process the wrong region and contribute to recognition errors.
We could improve the method by checking detected boundaries more carefully and
reducing the influence of covered areas. Since the dataset is relatively small, using
more varied images to refine the settings and then testing on separate images
would help us assess how well the method works under different conditions.
